<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Merchandising.Tests.Unit - domain and calculation tests. Spec section 7.

    Framework: MSTest, pinned in ADR-009 (ACCEPTED). Do not switch it.

    This file has been corrected from what `dotnet new mstest -lang VB`
    emits. The template produces a C# project file with the language
    swapped; ADR-009.2 records the details. Removed:
      <ImplicitUsings>  - C#-only concept, inert in Visual Basic
      <Nullable>        - C#-only concept, inert in Visual Basic
      <Using Include="..."/> - the C# global-using item. Visual Basic's
                        equivalent is <Import>. It was harmless only
                        because the template also writes a file-level
                        Imports line into every .vb it generates.
    And <TargetFramework> now uses $(MerchNetTfm) instead of the hard-coded
    net10.0 the template emits, so the solution moves together if the
    target is ever revised (Directory.Build.props).

    Parallelism: this project KEEPS the template's method-level
    parallelism (see MSTestSettings.vb). These tests touch no shared
    resource. The integration project deliberately does the opposite.
  -->


  <!--
    P1-15 retargeted this project from $(MerchNetTfm) to $(MerchWindowsTfm),
    and that needs a reason on the record.

    Merchandising.ClientCommon is net10.0-windows with UseWPF - deliberately,
    because spec section 7 puts converters, styles and shared XAML resources in
    it, and a plain net10.0 library cannot hold a ResourceDictionary. A net10.0
    test project cannot reference a net10.0-windows one, so P1-15's client
    behaviour (token never persisted, writes refused when the API is
    unreachable, correlation IDs propagated) had no place to be tested from.

    The alternative was a twelfth project purely for client tests. Retargeting
    costs nothing real: every component of this system is Windows-only already
    - WPF clients, a Windows Service, win-x64 packaging, XAMPP on Windows - so
    no portability is being given up, and P1-19's "both test projects" stays
    literally true.

    UseWPF is set because ClientCommon carries a WPF framework reference.
    Nothing in this project draws anything.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Merchandising.Tests.Unit</RootNamespace>
    <TargetFramework>$(MerchWindowsTfm)</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MSTest" Version="4.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Merchandising.Domain\Merchandising.Domain.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Merchandising.Contracts\Merchandising.Contracts.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Merchandising.ClientCommon\Merchandising.ClientCommon.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Merchandising.Inventory\Merchandising.Inventory.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Merchandising.Procurement\Merchandising.Procurement.vbproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Merchandising.POS\Merchandising.POS.vbproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
